      "id": "timer-03",
      "verdict": "supported",
      "line": 123,
      "fragment": "to run again, in which case it returns false.",
      "reason": "Once the callback has started and the timer has expired, Reset returns false and schedules the registered function again. A later Reset while that new execution is still pending can return true and reschedule it, as lines 121–123 specify.",
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "timer-04",
      "verdict": "contradicted",
      "line": 126,
      "fragment": "goroutine running f does not run concurrently with the prior",
      "reason": "Lines 124–128 explicitly withhold a guarantee against overlap when Reset returns false. The previous execution may still be running when the next one begins.",
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "timer-05",
      "verdict": "supported",
      "line": 67,
      "fragment": "It returns true if the call stops the timer",
      "reason": "AfterFunc gives the caller a timer whose Stop method can cancel the scheduled call (lines 174–175). A successful Stop on that still-pending timer returns true and prevents that execution. A second Stop without a Reset returns false under lines 67–68.",
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "timer-06",
      "verdict": "contradicted",
      "line": 178,
      "fragment": "newTimer(when(d), 0, goFunc, f, nil)",
      "reason": "If a caller variable holds f1 at registration, AfterFunc passes that function value as the timer argument. At expiration, goFunc invokes the stored value (line 182). Leaving the variable alone, registering a copy of its value, or rebinding only the caller variable to f2 after registration all yield invocation of f1. If f1 itself looks up mutable state, replacing or mutating that state can affect what f1 does while the timer still invokes f1.",
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "timer-07",
      "verdict": "unspecified",
      "line": 173,
      "fragment": "AfterFunc waits for the duration to elapse and then calls f",
      "reason": "The contract specifies when the registered callback runs and leaves application retry policy undefined.",
      "question": "OPEN: Should the application retry a timed-out operation, and if so, how many times?"
    }
